  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 330 PM CDT Tue Jul 28 2026\n\nCurrently through tonight...\n\nSeeing dry conditions across the forecast area this afternoon, \nfollowing a round of showers and thunderstorms which passed \nthrough last evening through early this morning. This \ndisturbance brought measurable rainfall to basically the entire \nforecast area...though a good chunk were only a few hundredths \nto around 0.15 of an inch. Main winners were along a narrow \nswath running roughly from Smith Center, KS up into southern \nYork County...where totals were closer to 0.4-1.0 inch (isolated\nspots exceeded 1 inch). This system helped cool temps a bit, \nand though dewpoints remain in the low-mid 70s...heat index \nvalues are an improvement over the past few days, sitting more \nin the 90s. Upper air and satellite data show little change in \nthe overall pattern across the CONUS...ridging continues to \ndominate the High Plains/central CONUS, keeping our flow west- \nnorthwesterly. The surface pattern across the area remains \npretty weak, which has kept winds for most locations on the \nlight/variable side. \n\nSeeing some scattered thunderstorms developing off to our west\nover the Rockies in CO up into the WY/NE border area, where \nsteeper lapse rates here at peak-heating lie. The WNW flow aloft\nwill push this activity east the rest of this \nafternoon/evening...although not very quickly. Kept the rest of \nthis afternoon into the evening hours dry...models are in decent\nagreement showing this activity gradually fading the further \neast it pushes, eventually losing daytime heating and there \nisn't quite as much instability as was in place the past few \ndays. After midnight, models are showing the potential for \nincreased storm development...running roughly WNW to ESE across \ncentral/southern portions of the forecast area, along increased \n(although still not strong) forcing along lower to mid-level \nboundaries/convergence. Models vary on just how much coverage \nthere is...kept the PoPs on the lower side (20-30 percent), but \nbroad. Not expecting this activity to be severe...though \ninstability remains in place, the overall forcing is weak and \ndeep layer shear looks to struggle to even get to 20kts. \n\nWednesday and Thursday...\n\nThe main story through the mid-week period continues to lie with\na couple more upper level shortwave disturbances passing \nthrough...overall there hasn't been any notable changes in the \nmodels/forecast. \n\nThink that Wednesday will be similar to today...in that there\ncould be some activity left over from tonight lingering around\nmainly southern/eastern portions of the area during the early-\nmid morning hours...with the majority of the daytime hours being\ndry. Not a lot of change in the upper level pattern, with the \npersistent ridging keeping that west-northwesterly flow in \nplace. At the surface, models are showing winds turning more \nsoutheasterly...with speeds around 10-15 MPH, some show the \npotential for gusts near 20 MPH in spots. Forecast highs also \nsimilar to today in the upper 80s to low 90s.\n\nLittle more uncertainty remains Wednesday night on through\nThursday night with the timing/coverage of precipitation \nassociated with additional disturbances moving through. Models \nvary more on Wed night into Thursday, some more generous with \nactivity, others showing a smaller swath developing with weaker \nforcing. Models remain pretty consistent showing a stronger wave\nmoving through Thu evening-night...bringing better potential \nfor storms, some of which could be strong-severe (the entire \narea remains in the SPC Day 3 Marginal Risk area). Forecast \nhighs a bit more of a range from the mid 80s-mid 90s...driven by\nsome models keeping activity lingering around longer into the \nday than others. \n\nFriday on into early next week...\n\nForecast for the end of the week into the start of the new work\nweek dries back out. The main impact from the disturbance \nmoving through Thursday night is with the westward shift of the \nmain upper level ridge axis, bringing more amplified north-\nnorthwesterly flow to the Central Plains at least through the \nweekend. Models showing an overall lack of disturbances through \nthe weekend, with some low PoPs returning around Tuesday. Breezy\nNNE winds are forecast for Friday in the wake of a sfc frontal \npassage...but at this point that front doesn't look to have a \nnotable downward impact on temps or dewpoints, with highs in the\nmid-80s to near 90. Forecast highs through the weekend on into \nMonday are similar to Friday, mainly in the 80s.",
